    {
      "id": "py401-md0",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "# Python 401 · Networking\n\nTalk to the network from Python — first with the **standard library** (always available), then with **`requests`** (install from the PyPI tab). We finish with a self-contained socket demo that needs no internet.\n\n**Prerequisites:** *Python 301*.\n\n> Cells that reach the internet are marked **🌐 needs internet**. The socket demo and URL parsing run fully offline.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-md1",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 1. Parsing URLs (offline)\n\n`urllib.parse` breaks apart and builds URLs — no network required.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-1",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "from urllib.parse import urlparse, urlencode, parse_qs\n\nurl = \"https://api.example.com/search?q=python&page=2&sort=new\"\n\n# urlparse() splits a URL into its six components and returns them as a named tuple,\n# so each piece is reachable by attribute instead of by string surgery.\nparts = urlparse(url)\nprint(\"scheme:\", parts.scheme)     # https        - the protocol\nprint(\"host:  \", parts.netloc)     # api.example.com - host, plus port and user info if present\nprint(\"path:  \", parts.path)       # /search      - everything before the \"?\"\n\n# parse_qs() turns \"q=python&page=2\" into a dict. Every value is a LIST, because a\n# query string may legitimately repeat a key (?tag=a&tag=b).\nprint(\"query: \", parse_qs(parts.query))\n\n# urlencode() goes the other way, and - importantly - percent-escapes anything unsafe,\n# so a value containing a space or \"&\" cannot corrupt the URL.\nbuilt = \"https://api.example.com/items?\" + urlencode({\"limit\": 10, \"tag\": \"new\"})\nprint(\"built: \", built)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-md2",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 2. HTTP with the standard library 🌐\n\n`urllib.request` performs HTTP with zero dependencies. This GET hits a public test API.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-2",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "import json\nfrom urllib.request import urlopen, Request\n\n# A Request object lets you attach headers. Many APIs reject requests without a\n# User-Agent, so setting one is a good habit even for simple GETs.\nreq = Request(\"https://httpbin.org/json\", headers={\"User-Agent\": \"Arima/1.0\"})\n\n# Network calls fail for reasons that have nothing to do with your code - no\n# connectivity, DNS, a proxy - so the whole block is guarded.\ntry:\n    # `with` closes the connection automatically, even if an error is raised inside.\n    # timeout=10 caps the wait; without it a hung server would block the cell forever.\n    with urlopen(req, timeout=10) as resp:\n        # json.load() reads from a file-like object (the response stream) and parses it.\n        # json.loads() - with the s - is the variant that takes a string.\n        data = json.load(resp)\n    print(\"status:\", resp.status)      # 200 means success\n    barista.display(data)              # pretty-print the parsed JSON\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Network unavailable or blocked:\", e)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-md3",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 3. The `requests` library 🌐\n\n`requests` is the de-facto HTTP client. **Install it first:** open **Packages → PyPI** and install `requests`, then run this cell.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-3",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "# requests is not in the standard library, so importing it may fail. Catching that\n# specific error keeps the cell green on a machine where it has not been installed.\ntry:\n    import requests\nexcept ModuleNotFoundError:\n    print(\"requests not installed - add it from Packages -> PyPI, then re-run.\")\nelse:\n    # `else` on a try block runs only when NO exception was raised - the import worked.\n    #   params=      builds the query string for you, escaping as needed\n    #   timeout=10   applies to both connecting and reading\n    r = requests.get(\"https://httpbin.org/get\", params={\"tool\": \"arima\"}, timeout=10)\n\n    print(\"status:\", r.status_code)          # requests exposes the code as an int\n    # .json() parses the response body in one step - no json.loads() needed.\n    # httpbin echoes your query string back under \"args\", which is what we print.\n    print(\"echoed args:\", r.json().get(\"args\"))",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-md4",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 4. A local TCP server + client (offline)\n\nSockets are the foundation of all networking. Here a tiny server and client talk over `localhost` in one cell — no internet needed.",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-4",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "import socket, threading\n\n# The server has to run at the same time as the client - it blocks while waiting for a\n# connection - so it goes in its own thread and reports back through `ready`.\ndef server(ready):\n    # AF_INET = IPv4, SOCK_STREAM = TCP (the reliable, ordered, connection-based kind).\n    srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n\n    # SO_REUSEADDR lets the port be re-bound immediately after a previous run, instead\n    # of waiting out the operating system's TIME_WAIT period.\n    srv.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_REUSEADDR, 1)\n\n    # Port 0 means \"any free port\" - the OS picks one, which avoids clashing with\n    # whatever else is running on this machine.\n    srv.bind((\"127.0.0.1\", 0))\n    srv.listen(1)                       # start queueing incoming connections (backlog of 1)\n\n    ready[\"port\"] = srv.getsockname()[1]  # ask the OS which port it actually chose\n    ready[\"evt\"].set()                    # signal the main thread that the port is known\n\n    conn, addr = srv.accept()           # BLOCKS here until the client connects\n    msg = conn.recv(1024).decode()      # read up to 1024 bytes, then bytes -> str\n    conn.sendall(f\"echo: {msg}\".encode())  # str -> bytes; sockets only speak bytes\n    conn.close(); srv.close()\n\n# An Event is a thread-safe flag: one thread waits on it, another sets it.\nready = {\"evt\": threading.Event()}\n\n# daemon=True means this thread will not keep the process alive on its own.\nt = threading.Thread(target=server, args=(ready,), daemon=True)\nt.start()\nready[\"evt\"].wait(5)                    # wait (up to 5s) until the port has been assigned\n\n# create_connection() resolves the address and connects in one call.\nclient = socket.create_connection((\"127.0.0.1\", ready[\"port\"]), timeout=5)\nclient.sendall(b\"hello sockets\")        # the b\"\" prefix makes a bytes literal\nprint(client.recv(1024).decode())       # read the reply and decode it back to text\nclient.close(); t.join(timeout=5)       # join() waits for the server thread to finish",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-md5",
      "type": "MARKDOWN",
      "source": "## 5. Concurrent requests 🌐\n\n`ThreadPoolExecutor` fetches several URLs in parallel — much faster than one at a time. (Needs internet.)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-5",
      "type": "CODE",
      "mode": "python",
      "source": "import json, time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nfrom urllib.request import urlopen\n\n# httpbin's /delay/N endpoint sleeps N seconds before replying - a controlled way to\n# show that four one-second requests need not take four seconds.\nurls = [f\"https://httpbin.org/delay/1?i={i}\" for i in range(4)]\n\ndef fetch(u):\n    try:\n        with urlopen(u, timeout=15) as r:\n            return r.status\n    except Exception as e:\n        # Return the error rather than raising it: one dead URL should not abort the\n        # whole batch, and pool.map re-raises anything a worker throws.\n        return f\"err: {e}\"\n\nstart = time.perf_counter()\ntry:\n    # The pool runs up to 4 workers at once. `with` shuts it down and waits for the\n    # in-flight work when the block ends.\n    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool:\n        # pool.map() is like the built-in map(), except the calls run concurrently.\n        # Results still come back in the order of the input list.\n        results = list(pool.map(fetch, urls))\n\n    print(\"results:\", results)\n    # Roughly 1 second in total rather than 4 - the requests overlap. Threads work here\n    # because the time is spent WAITING on the network, not using the CPU.\n    print(f\"4 x 1s requests in {time.perf_counter() - start:.2f}s (parallel)\")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Network unavailable:\", e)",
      "output": "",
      "executionCount": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "py401-md6",
